"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The preoperative PSA was elevated at 12.3. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and pathological stage pT2 with no extraprostatic extension. Positive surgical margins and absence of seminal vesicle invasion were noted. A key uncertainty is the nodal status, as no lymph nodes were removed, and missing nodal sampling widens this uncertainty. Biopsy results showed a progression from Gleason 3+3 to 3+4 over time.\n\nCountervailing factors include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the absence of lymph node involvement documented in the pathology report. The postoperative PSA trajectory and subsequent follow-up are unknown due to missing previous notes. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory would significantly revise this prognosis. The current heuristic horizon suggests a follow-up period around 47 months without an event. The fixed estimated horizon is 50.00 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 30.00–70.00 months; its coverage has not been validated."
